{"type":"user","uuid":"8e8d7897-35d4-45a9-8054-32fe371948d7","session_id":"626f5182-b9a4-4090-abf6-af597cd80f07","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_55d37a4ef8a3416180dfe1b9","is_error":false,"content":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\"?>\n\n<FrameLayout xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\"\n             xmlns:tools=\"http://schemas.android.com/tools\"\n             android:orientation=\"vertical\"\n             android:layout_width=\"match_parent\"\n             android:layout_height=\"match_parent\">\n\n    <android.support.v7.widget.RecyclerView\n        android:id=\"@android:id/list\"\n        android:layout_width=\"match_parent\"\n        android:layout_height=\"match_parent\"\n        android:scrollbarStyle=\"outsideOverlay\"\n        android:paddingTop=\"@dimen/list_vertical_padding\"\n        android:paddingBottom=\"@dimen/list_vertical_padding\"\n        android:clipToPadding=\"false\"\n        tools:listitem=\"@layout/new_episodes_listitem\"\n        tools:itemCount=\"13\"/>\n\n    <ProgressBar\n        android:id=\"@+id/progLoading\"\n        android:layout_width=\"wrap_content\"\n        android:layout_height=\"wrap_content\"\n        android:layout_gravity=\"center\"\n        android:indeterminateOnly=\"true\"\n        android:visibility=\"gone\"\n        tools:visibility=\"gone\"\n        tools:layout_width=\"match_parent\"\n        tools:layout_height=\"64dp\"\n        tools:background=\"@android:color/holo_red_light\"/>\n\n</FrameLayout>"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"7fb97399-295f-4314-aee8-5a0a4dd8c427","session_id":"626f5182-b9a4-4090-abf6-af597cd80f07","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"7fb97399-295f-4314-aee8-5a0a4dd8c427","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the structure:\n\n1. **QueueFragment** uses `queue_fragment.xml` with:\n   - A `RecyclerView` (`@+id/recyclerView`) that fills the parent\n   - An empty TextView (`@id/android:empty`) overlaid on top\n   - The empty view is controlled manually via `txtvEmpty.setVisibility()`\n\n2. **AllEpisodesFragment** (parent of NewEpisodesFragment and FavoriteEpisodesFragment) uses `all_episodes_fragment.xml` with:\n   - A `RecyclerView` (`@android:id/list`)\n   - No explicit empty view in the layout\n\nThe bug manifests when removing the last item from a list — the empty view gets shown but the removed item's Snackbar undo action fails because the item appears to shift off-screen. This points to a race condition where the UI state updates before the RecyclerView fully processes the item removal, leaving the snackbar's anchor point in an invalid position. Looking at the `all_episodes_fragment.xml` layout, I notice there's no empty view defined in the XML—the code only sets visibility for the RecyclerView and loading progress bar, so when the episode list becomes empty, nothing replaces it visually. Yet the bug report mentions an empty view being displayed, which suggests the RecyclerView's built-in empty state mechanism might be triggering automatically through some framework behavior. the item, and then DB events fire which remove it from the queue list. The issue is that `onSwiped` gets called during the swipe animation, but the actual removal from the adapter happens asynchronously through the `REMOVED` event. This timing mismatch could mean the adapter state and the data source get out of sync—the item gets removed from the UI but not yet from the queue list, or vice versa. The `onSwiped` callback removes the item from the queue at that position, but the adapter hasn't been notified of this change yet, so there's a mismatch between what the UI shows and what the data source contains. When the last item is swiped away, `notifyItemRemoved(0)` triggers the animation, and `onFragmentLoaded(false)` begins toggling visibility between the empty state and the recycler view. The snackbar is created with the position captured before the removal, but if the item is the final one in the queue, this could create a timing issue where the position becomes invalid as the view disappears. the item is being removed from the database before the UI updates, which could cause a race condition. Looking at `NewEpisodesFragment`, it calls `DBWriter.markItemPlayed()` to remove the item asynchronously, then shows a snackbar with an undo action that would re-add it. The problem might be that if the UI updates before the snackbar's undo callback fires, the position tracking gets out of sync.\n\nLet me trace through `QueueFragment` more carefully — the snackbar is created with the item before the database removal happens, and the undo action re-queues it at the captured position. But I need to check if there's a layout issue or if the snackbar itself is getting hidden when the empty view appears. I see the issue now—the ItemTouchHelper captures the position when the swipe begins, but then `onEventMainThread(QueueEvent event)` receives a REMOVED event and calls `notifyItemRemoved(position)` while the item is still mid-swipe animation. When the Snackbar's undo action tries to add the item back at that position, the RecyclerView is in an inconsistent state because the item removal notification has already been processed, potentially causing the newly re-added item to appear off-screen or in the wrong place. The timing issue is that `DBWriter.removeQueueItem()` fires the REMOVED event through `EventDistributor`, which triggers `onEventMainThread()` to upda